course: dictionary
lesson: 12596
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many everyday words have more than one meaning, and discharge is one of them. To find the right meaning, read the words around it. Those words are its context, and they leave clues.
explain: «The nurse said Sam could be discharged from hospital today.» Here discharge means to let someone leave officially. The clue is hospital, a place where people are looked after until they are well enough to go home.
explain: «The pipe discharges dirty water into the river.» Here discharge means to let something flow out. The clues are water and river. Swap in your meaning: «The pipe lets dirty water out into the river» still makes sense, so you have it right.
fact: Discharge can be a verb (an action word) or a noun (a naming word).
fact: As a verb, discharge can mean to let someone leave, to let out a liquid, gas or electricity, to do a duty fully, or to fire a gun.
fact: A battery discharges when it loses its stored electricity.
fact: Lightning is an electric discharge.
fact: The word looks and sounds the same in every meaning, so always use the context to decide which one is right.
exercise: «The patient was discharged this morning.» What does discharged mean here? | To be sent to bed for a rest | To be given a new name | *To be let leave hospital | To be painted a bright colour
exercise: «A battery discharges when it loses its stored power.» What does discharges mean here? | Sings a loud song | *Lets out its stored electricity | Turns into water | Gets bigger and heavier
exercise: «She discharged her duties well.» What does discharged mean here? | *Carried out fully | Forgot all about | Fired a gun | Threw away
test: «The pipe discharges water into the sea.» What does discharges mean? | Freezes | Stops | Counts | *Lets out
test: In «The cut left a clear discharge», what is the discharge? | *A liquid that comes out of the cut | A loud bang from a gun | A doctor's order to go home | A soldier leaving the army
exam: Which meaning fits «The soldier was discharged from the army»? | To do a duty fully | To let out a liquid | *To let someone leave officially | To fire a gun
